========================================================================
README for the Windows port of the Oculus Rift CV1 camera viewer
========================================================================

Overview
========

This is a Windows port of Oliver Kreylos' Oculus Rift CV1 camera code.
It talks to a CV1 tracking camera ("Rift Sensor", USB VID 2833, PID
0211) through its low-level USB interface using libusb-1.0 on top of
Microsoft's inbox WinUSB driver, with no Vrui dependency. The port
captures 1280x960 8-bit grayscale frames and writes them out as PNG
files.

Easy install (one MSI, no background programs)
==============================================

Download RiftSensorCam.msi from the latest release:

  https://github.com/Graicc/OculusRiftCV1Camera/releases/latest

Plug in the camera, then run the MSI. It installs a self-contained DirectShow virtual
camera, "Rift Sensor Camera", that opens the Rift sensor directly
whenever an application starts using the camera - there is no
companion program to run. The installer also takes care of the driver:
it binds Microsoft's inbox WinUSB driver to the sensor's video
interface (the same as Device Manager's manual driver selection; no
third-party drivers, no test signing) and stops the Oculus runtime
service, which would otherwise hold the camera.

Configuration lives in HKLM\SOFTWARE\RiftSensorCam:
  TargetBrightness (default 80)  Software auto-exposure target, 16-250
  Rotate (default 0)             Clockwise image rotation: 0/90/180/270

Uninstalling from "Apps & features" unregisters the camera and reverts
the sensor to its original Oculus driver.

If the camera was not plugged in during installation, plug it in and
run (elevated): "C:\Program Files\RiftSensorCam\RiftSensorSetup.exe" install

Building the MSI yourself: GitHub Actions builds it on every push
(.github\workflows\build.yml, artifact "RiftSensorCam-msi"), and
attaches it to a release for v* tags. That workflow is also the
reference for a local build; it boils down to:

1. Fetch libusb's MSVC static build into libusb-msvc\ (the workflow
   downloads libusb-1.0.29.7z and copies include\libusb.h to
   include\libusb-1.0\libusb.h).
2. From an x64 MSVC prompt, compile the filter DLL in filter\ and
   RiftSensorSetup.exe in installer\ (see the two "cl" invocations in
   the workflow).
3. Compile installer\Product.wxs with WiX v3.14:
   candle -arch x64 Product.wxs && light -o RiftSensorCam.msi Product.wixobj

The sections below describe the original manual setup (standalone
capture tool and sender-based virtual webcam); they remain useful for
development and debugging.

Files:

  OculusRiftCV1CameraWin.cpp  The port: ESP770U controller init, AR0134
                              sensor setup, UVC probe/commit and
                              isochronous streaming via libusb, frame
                              reassembly, PNG output (stb_image_write).
  OculusRiftCV1CameraWebcam.cpp
                              Virtual webcam sender: same camera code,
                              but streams frames continuously into the
                              UnityCapture DirectShow filter so the
                              camera appears as "Rift Sensor Camera" in
                              any app (Zoom, Discord, OBS, browsers).
  unitycapture/               Prebuilt UnityCapture DirectShow filter
                              (https://github.com/schellingb/UnityCapture,
                              zlib license) and its shared-memory
                              protocol header (shared.inl).
  winusb-assign.cpp           Helper that binds the inbox "WinUsb
                              Device" driver (winusb.inf) to the
                              sensor's video interface, replicating
                              Device Manager's manual driver selection.
                              Run elevated, once.
  winusbtest.cpp              Small probe that checks whether a device
                              path can be driven with the WinUsb API.
  stb_image_write.h           Single-header PNG writer (public domain).

Prerequisites
=============

1. MSYS2 (https://www.msys2.org, or `winget install MSYS2.MSYS2`), then
   from an MSYS2 shell:

   $ pacman -S mingw-w64-x86_64-gcc mingw-w64-x86_64-libusb

Building
========

From a MINGW64 shell in this directory:

$ g++ -O2 -o OculusRiftCV1CameraWin.exe OculusRiftCV1CameraWin.cpp \
      -lusb-1.0 -static-libgcc -static-libstdc++
$ g++ -O2 -o winusb-assign.exe winusb-assign.cpp -lsetupapi -static

Copy libusb-1.0.dll and libwinpthread-1.dll from C:\msys64\mingw64\bin
next to OculusRiftCV1CameraWin.exe if you run it outside an MSYS2
shell.

One-time driver setup
=====================

Windows binds the sensor's video interface (MI_00) to Oculus' own
driver (OCUSBVID), which libusb cannot use. The winusb-assign helper
force-assigns Microsoft's signed inbox WinUSB driver instead — the
programmatic equivalent of Device Manager's "Update driver -> Let me
pick" flow. No third-party drivers or certificates are installed.

From an elevated PowerShell:

> Stop-Service OVRService          # Oculus runtime holds the sensor
> .\winusb-assign.exe              # binds "WinUsb Device" to MI_00
> New-ItemProperty -Path "HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Enum\USB\VID_2833&PID_0211&MI_00\<instance>\Device Parameters" `
      -Name DeviceInterfaceGUIDs -PropertyType MultiString `
      -Value @("{b35924d6-3e16-4a9e-9782-5524a4b79bac}") -Force
> pnputil /restart-device "USB\VID_2833&PID_0211&MI_00\<instance>"

(winusb-assign.exe defaults to the device instance ID of the sensor it
was written for; pass your sensor's MI_00 instance ID as the first
argument if it differs — find it in Device Manager under
Details -> Device instance path, or with:
  Get-PnpDevice | ? { $_.InstanceId -like "USB\VID_2833&PID_0211&MI_00*" } )

The DeviceInterfaceGUIDs registry value is required for libusb to
locate the device; the GUID value itself is arbitrary.

To restore the Oculus driver later: Device Manager -> Universal Serial
Bus devices -> "WinUsb Device" -> Update driver -> "Search
automatically for drivers". The Oculus driver package (oem*.inf) is
still installed and outranks winusb.inf, so Windows rebinds it.

Use
===

> .\OculusRiftCV1CameraWin.exe [-auto] [-gain <0-255>]
      [-exposure <lines>] [-frames <n>] [-o <prefix>] [--usbdk]

  -auto             Enable the sensor's automatic exposure/gain control
  -gain <g>         Global gain, 0-255 (default 128)
  -exposure <e>     Exposure time in sensor line periods (default 800);
                    values above 960 trade frame rate for brightness,
                    useful in IR-dark rooms
  -frames <n>       Number of frames to capture (default 5)
  -o <prefix>       Output file name prefix (default "frame"; writes
                    <prefix>00.png, <prefix>01.png, ...)
  --usbdk           Use libusb's UsbDk backend instead of WinUSB (only
                    if you installed UsbDk and rebooted; not needed
                    with the driver setup above)

Make sure the Oculus runtime is not holding the camera
(Stop-Service OVRService from an elevated prompt) before running.

Using the camera as a webcam
============================

One-time filter registration (elevated):

> regsvr32 /s unitycapture\UnityCaptureFilter64.dll "/i:UnityCaptureName=Rift Sensor Camera"

(Unregister later with: regsvr32 /u /s unitycapture\UnityCaptureFilter64.dll)

Build the sender:

$ g++ -O2 -o OculusRiftCV1CameraWebcam.exe OculusRiftCV1CameraWebcam.cpp \
      -lusb-1.0 -static-libgcc -static-libstdc++

Then run it (and leave it running) whenever you want the webcam:

> .\OculusRiftCV1CameraWebcam.exe [-target <0-255>] [-gain <0-255>]
      [-exposure <lines>] [-auto] [-rotate <0|90|180|270>]

"Rift Sensor Camera" (1280x960) then shows up in every app that
enumerates DirectShow cameras. Stop with Ctrl+C.

By default the sender runs a software auto-exposure loop that steers
sensor exposure and gain toward a target mean brightness (default 80;
change with -target). Passing -gain or -exposure switches to fixed
manual settings; -auto uses the sensor's hardware auto-exposure
instead (not recommended: it caps exposure at one frame time, which is
too dark for indoor scenes). The -rotate option rotates the image
clockwise in case the sensor is mounted sideways.

Quick self-test with ffmpeg:

$ ffmpeg -f dshow -video_size 1280x960 -i video="Rift Sensor Camera" \
      -vf "select=gte(n\,90)" -frames:v 1 check.png

Notes
=====

- The camera streams over a SuperSpeed isochronous endpoint; plug it
  into a USB 3.0 port.
- Frames are raw sensor luminance. The sensor mostly sees infrared;
  daylight scenes look like black-and-white photographs, and IR LEDs
  (e.g. Rift headset, remotes) appear as bright dots.
- While WinUSB is bound to the sensor, the Oculus software will not
  see that sensor. Restore the driver (see above) to use it for
  tracking again.
- The Linux version and original documentation are in README.
